WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.420
ERK was found by following an activity.

2
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.140
Melanie Cobb's laboratory followed a kinase activity that insulin switched on.

3
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.540
They ran it through eight chromatography columns, cloned its gene, and named the

4
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:24.460
enzyme ERK. Its sequence resembled two yeast kinases that respond to mating pheromone.

5
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:31.643
Then Natalie Ahn, in Edwin Krebs's laboratory, asked what switches ERK on.

6
00:00:32.743 --> 00:00:38.703
In 1991 she found two activities in extracts of cells treated with growth factor.

7
00:00:39.763 --> 00:00:43.703
Both switched on inactive MAP kinase in a test tube.

8
00:00:43.703 --> 00:00:48.163
Was ERK switching itself on, or did something else do it?

9
00:00:48.983 --> 00:00:51.603
Here is the reasoning, step by step.

10
00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:55.603
The activator and ERK run down a column.

11
00:00:56.463 --> 00:01:00.663
If the activator comes apart from ERK there, it is a different protein.

12
00:01:01.663 --> 00:01:04.003
Then ERK was not switching itself on.

13
00:01:04.817 --> 00:01:08.017
Ahn purified the activator with Rony Seger.

14
00:01:08.597 --> 00:01:12.357
It phosphorylates ERK on both threonine and tyrosine.

15
00:01:13.257 --> 00:01:16.937
Several laboratories reached this enzyme at about the same time.

16
00:01:17.357 --> 00:01:18.917
It is now called MEK.

17
00:01:19.637 --> 00:01:23.577
MEK is the kinase that sits between Raf and ERK.

18
00:01:24.377 --> 00:01:26.157
ERK needs both phosphates.

19
00:01:26.757 --> 00:01:29.317
Take either one off, and ERK goes quiet.

20
00:01:29.317 --> 00:01:33.717
An activator that comes apart from ERK is a different protein.

